1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:09,020
Okay. So, uh, for today's deep dive, we are mapping out what is essentially the ultimate playbook for a really high stakes meeting.

2
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:10,200
Yeah, it's a tough one.

3
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:20,300
It is. We're looking at Audio Brief KP1 Module 1, Video 1.7, and the mission here is to figure out the exact commitments you need to secure from your

4
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:23,840
minister to build a national enterprise architecture.

5
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:32,820
Right. Because if you're a government official or, you know, CDO preparing to walk into your minister's office, you probably just spent weeks pitching this idea.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,680
Pitching the value of treating government services as reusable digital components.

7
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,260
Exactly. And let's say they finally agree.

8
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,440
They wanna know what you need to start tomorrow.

9
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:45,110
Which is exciting, but also, well, it's terrifying.

10
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:50,550
Yeah. Because if you ask for the wrong things in that exact moment, the initiative is just gonna fail before it even begins.

11
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:55,520
Oh, absolutely. To do this properly, you have to walk out of that room with four very specific commitments.

12
00:00:55,840 --> 00:01:01,150
So let's get into it. First ask, you need a dedicated standing team of two to four senior architects.

13
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,370
But, uh, I mean, ministers will usually push back on that, right?

14
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:05,360
Oh, almost always.

15
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:10,440
Yeah. They'll suggest outsourcing it to temporary consultants so they don't have to increase permanent headcount.

16
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:11,460
Why is that a bad idea?

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,080
Well, because this team has to act as your country's own permanent muscle

18
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,700
for cross-cutting digital decisions.

19
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:20,180
Permanent muscle, I like that.

20
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:28,560
Yeah. And if temporary consultants just build your framework and then, you know, leave when the contract ends, your institutional knowledge just vanishes entirely.

21
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:34,200
Right. The team has to exist whether or not any single delivery program is currently running.

22
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:35,670
Okay, so you have your architects,

23
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:40,340
but a team without actual authority can't enforce anything.

24
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:42,720
Which brings us to the second ask.

25
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:45,860
You must establish an enterprise architecture board.

26
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:50,960
Yes, and this board should be chaired by your CDO or even the minister directly.

27
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:53,760
And it has to include the owners of the major state registers.

28
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:57,410
Registers, not registries, just to be precise with the terminology.

29
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:05,280
Right. But the really crucial detail here, and you have to be exceptionally clear with your minister about this, is that the board's mandate must be binding,

30
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,620
not advisory.

31
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:08,100
Because if it's just advisory-

32
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:17,810
Then they have no real power. They need the explicit authority to review new digital projects and actually enforce architectural boundaries across different

33
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:18,560
ministries.

34
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:22,740
Yeah, 'cause if they only offer suggestions, ministries will just completely ignore them.

35
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:27,820
Exactly. And your EA just becomes unread documentation gathering dust.

36
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,920
Okay, so you've got the people and the power,

37
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:32,900
but authority needs capital to execute.

38
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:34,640
So third ask,

39
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:40,200
you need a sustained five-year envelope making up about 2% of the digital budget.

40
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:47,140
Right. And to get that first phase going, you tell your minister you need 10 to 15 senior person months allocated immediately.

41
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:49,280
But why the five-year envelope specifically?

42
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,840
Because digital transformation doesn't just, you know, wrap up neatly at the end of a fiscal year.

43
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:55,870
Mm-hmm. You need consistent funding. Yeah.

44
00:02:55,880 --> 00:03:00,820
That 2% investment is the leverage that makes the other 98% of your budget run efficiently.

45
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,160
Wow. Okay. So the funding is secured, but the brief mentions one final threat, the fourth ask.

46
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:14,600
The firewall. Exactly. The promise. You have to secure a promise from your minister that the team will not be pulled onto the urgent project of the week.

47
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,440
Listen, this is honestly the most critical commitment.

48
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:22,240
You have to let that sink in. The team will not be pulled onto the urgent project of the week.

49
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:23,370
It's that serious?

50
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:27,390
It is. Because ministers face this constant intense pressure

51
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,440
to deliver highly visible public services quickly.

52
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,480
So foundational work just gets cannibalized.

53
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:34,940
So they just borrow the architects?

54
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,020
Right. In their second year, architecture teams are routinely reassigned to rescue some flagship delivery project that's failing,

55
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:45,680
and when that happens, the architecture work just completely stops.

56
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,140
You have to get this protection explicitly agreed upon.

57
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:53,810
Okay, so you have these four asks on a single page: team, board, budget, promise,

58
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:58,240
but then you have to manage the minister's expectations about time.

59
00:03:58,280 --> 00:03:59,420
Yeah, the uncomfortable truth.

60
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:02,510
Right. Because these timelines totally conflict with election cycles.

61
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:08,140
Six months to an approved roadmap, 18 to 24 months to a fully operating practice.

62
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:10,060
And five years to achieve mature governance.

63
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:16,220
Five years. The minister authorizing this work today probably won't even be in office to see it completed.

64
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:18,180
It's a huge political paradox.

65
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:20,640
It is, but frame it as a litmus test.

66
00:04:22,079 --> 00:04:26,480
A minister who sees that timeline as a problem just wants a quick deliverable.

67
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,400
Right. But a minister who accepts it,

68
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:31,720
they understand they are building lasting infrastructure.

69
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:36,060
Okay. So to sum up, bring all four asks to the meeting together.

70
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:38,560
Without any one of them, the work is gonna struggle.

71
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:45,950
And to help your minister understand how this operates in practice, there are four countries to point to which we'll actually examine in this video.

72
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:56,040
Perfect. But before you walk into that office, consider this: If true digital architecture outlasts political cycles, how might building this permanent muscle fundamentally

73
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:59,080
change how governments think about their decades long legacy?

74
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,680
Sources are in the video description.
